.TH MDBX_DEFRAG 1 "2026-07-07" "MDBX 0.14"
.SH NAME
mdbx_defrag \- MDBX database defragmentation tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdbx_defrag
[\c
.BR \-V ]
[\c
.BR \-v [ v [ v [ ... ]]]]
[\c
.BR \-q ]
[\c
.BR \-1 | \-2 | \-3 | \-4 | \-5 | \-6 | \-7 | \-8 | \-9 ]
[\c
.BI \-t \ seconds\fR]
[\c
.BI \-f \ percent\fR]
[\c
.BI \-r \ percent\fR]
[\c
.BI \-s \ megabytes\fR]
[\c
.BR \-c ]
[\c
.BR \-u ]
[\c
.BR \-U ]
.BR \ db_pathname
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B mdbx_defrag
utility performs online defragmentation of an MDBX database,
moving pages to pack the database and reclaiming unused space.
The database file is shrunk as pages become free, reducing its
size on disk.
Defragmentation proceeds in cycles while the environment is
opened for writing; it may be limited by a number of cycles,
a duration, or by the amount of space to be freed.
The database remains fully usable during defragmentation,
both for reading and for concurrent writes by other processes.
.PP
By default the environment is opened in exclusive/monopolistic
read-write mode. If the environment is already used by another
process(s), the tool falls back to cooperative mode.
The cooperative mode could be forced by the
.B \-c
option.
.PP
Several interacting processes may run defragmentation in
cooperative mode simultaneously; only a single one performs
actual work at a time while the others wait for their turn.
.PP
Defragmentation may be interrupted by sending a signal such as
SIGINT (Ctrl-C) to the utility. The process aborts gracefully,
preserving the database consistency, and reports the reason of
stopping as well as the amount of already performed work.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-V
Write the library version number to the standard output, and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v
Produce more verbose output. Could be repeated up to 9 times for
extra details from debug-enabled builds.
.TP
.BR \-q
Be quiet; do not output anything except errors.
.TP
.BR \-1
Perform a single quick defragmentation cycle without two-stage moves.
.TP
.BR \-2 | \-3 | \-4 | \-5 | \-6 | \-7 | \-8 | \-9
Limit the number of defragmentation cycles to the given value.
.TP
.BI \-t \ seconds
Limit the duration of defragmentation to the given number of seconds.
.TP
.BI \-f \ percent
Defragment until free up the given percentage of unused space.
The value must be in the range from 1 to 100 (100 by default).
.TP
.BI \-r \ percent
Acceptable undefragmented residue in percent of the used space
(0 by default). The value must be in the range from 1 to 100.
.TP
.BI \-s \ megabytes
Preferred defragmentation step/transaction size, i.e. the maximal
amount of pages processed per step, in megabytes.
.TP
.BR \-c
Force cooperative mode while opening the environment, i.e. don't
try to open it in exclusive/monopolistic mode.
.TP
.BR \-u
Warm up the database before defragmentation via notifying the OS
kernel of subsequent access to the database pages.
.TP
.BR \-U
Warm up the database before defragmentation, notifying the OS
kernel of subsequent access to the database pages, then forcibly
load ones by sequential access and tries to lock database pages
in memory.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is zero if no errors occur.
Errors result in a non-zero exit status and
a diagnostic message being written to standard error.
Successful but incomplete defragmentation (stopped due to a time
limit, a cycles limit, a threshold or an interference with readers)
is not considered an error, but the reason of stopping is reported.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
